\DOC ASSIGNS_SWAP_TAC

\TYPE {ASSIGNS_SWAP_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves goals asserting that the sequential composition of two {ASSIGNS}
relations commutes, closing them outright.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal whose conclusion equates
{ASSIGNS t ,, ASSIGNS u} with {ASSIGNS u ,, ASSIGNS t}, it discharges the goal:
{
   ?- ASSIGNS t ,, ASSIGNS u = ASSIGNS u ,, ASSIGNS t
   =============================================  ASSIGNS_SWAP_TAC
}
\noindent Success depends on establishing that the two state components
involved are non-interfering, which it decides by a component-orthogonality
computation on {t} and {u}. The components may be plain state components or
ones built by composition with subcomponent selectors, including memory regions
over computed addresses.

\FAILURE
Fails when the orthogonality computation cannot classify the pair of components
supplied in the conclusion — for example when they are nested or overlapping
views of the same underlying state — raising a failure from that internal
component analysis rather than returning a proof state.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, the goals have this commuted-composition shape with no
assumptions, and the only successful outcome is the goal being closed.

\ENDDOC
